## 3. Deviation safety assessment

Documented deviation: default build path uses StubWorldRuntime; real WorldRuntime compiles only under UseProjectReferences=true.

Assessment: ACCEPTABLE and safely implemented.
- The split is compile-time via #if USE_PROJECT_REFERENCES, not runtime branching. The real WorldRuntime cannot silently hide — it literally does not compile under default build.
- WorldRuntimeFactory.Create() is the single selection point; #if selects WorldRuntime vs StubWorldRuntime.
- The test project mirrors the same define, so tests only run under UseProjectReferences=true. Under default build, App.World.Tests yields 0 tests (confirmed by run).
- This means `dotnet test project/FantaSim.sln` (default) will NOT exercise App.World tests. Only `dotnet test -p:UseProjectReferences=true` runs them. This is a known consequence of the deviation and is acceptable because the local feed lacks FantaSim.World.* packages — the alternative (pack-to-feed) is explicitly out of scope per plan must-not-have.
- The plan's success criteria #3 says "dotnet test project/FantaSim.sln exits 0" — this will pass (0 tests in App.World.Tests is not a failure), but the App.World tests only run under the project-ref flag. This is consistent with the documented deviation.

Risk: A future operator running `dotnet test project/FantaSim.sln` without the flag will see App.World.Tests report "No test is available" and may misread this as a discovery failure rather than the expected conditional-compilation outcome. Mitigation: the test project csproj has a clear comment explaining this.

